e. The 1950s hold the most record highs of any decade (48). Grand Forks' all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on August 7, 1983.","t_chg":"Grand Forks has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 82 complete years: Grand Forks' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.38\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.21 to +0.56), about +3.2\u00b0F since 1942. Grand Forks' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.33\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +0.53), about +2.7\u00b0F since 1942.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Grand Forks has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Grand Forks' yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.34 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.03 to +0.65), about +2.8 inches since 1942. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 21.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 2019 (28.11 in); the driest, 1963 (10.65 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}